II. The Wonder Field — Formal Definition

2.1 The Latent Variable

The Wonder Field is a hypothetical latent variable representing the multi-dimensional state of audience experience:

\mathcal{W}_{\text{latent}} = f(S, P, U, I, E, C)

Where:

\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{Symbol} & \text{Component} & \text{Definition} \\
\hline
S(t) & \text{Surprise} & \text{Violation of expectations} \\
P(t) & \text{Prediction Error} & \text{Magnitude of deviation from anticipated outcomes} \\
U(t) & \text{Uncertainty} & \text{Resistance to immediate explanation} \\
I(t) & \text{Emotional Involvement} & \text{Depth of affective engagement} \\
E(t) & \text{Emergence} & \text{Perception of novel properties from interaction} \\
C(t) & \text{Cognitive Overload} & \text{Saturation of processing capacity}
\end{array}

Each component is itself a construct requiring operational definition and measurement validation. The framework does not assume these definitions are correct; it proposes them as starting points for empirical investigation.

2.2 The Triple Distinction

The most critical conceptual clarification:

\boxed{
\text{Observed Response} \neq \text{Subjective Wonder} \neq \text{Model Estimate}
}

Observed Response: What can be measured—movement, silence, gaze, facial expression, physiological signals, questionnaire responses.

Subjective Wonder: What the audience actually experiences—their internal state, accessible only through self-report and imperfectly even then.

Model Estimate: What the system computes as its best guess of the audience's wonder state, based on observed responses and prior data.

These three are related but not identical. The model estimate may be wrong. The observed response may not reflect subjective wonder. The self-report may be biased, unreliable, or influenced by social desirability.

The entire scientific program depends on understanding the relationships between these three levels. This understanding cannot be assumed; it must be established empirically.

2.3 The Primary Outcome

The primary outcome measure is direct self-report:

\text{SWS} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} \text{item}_j

Where SWS is the Subjective Wonder Score, computed as the mean of n items adapted from validated awe scales. The items assess felt wonder, perceived vastness, and need for accommodation.

The model estimate is an exploratory outcome:

\mathcal{W}_{\text{model}}(t) = \sum_{i} w_i z_i(t) - \lambda z_C(t)

Where z_i(t) are standardized scores for each component and w_i, \lambda are weights estimated from data.

The validation question is:

\boxed{
\text{Does } \mathcal{W}_{\text{model}} \text{ predict } \mathcal{W}_{\text{self-report}} \text{ at } r \geq 0.4?
}

If not, the measurement approach fails even if experimental hypotheses are supported.

2.4 The Nonlinearity Hypothesis

The relationship between complexity and wonder is hypothesized to be non-monotonic:

\frac{\partial \mathcal{W}}{\partial X} > 0 \quad \text{for} \quad X < X^*

\frac{\partial \mathcal{W}}{\partial X} < 0 \quad \text{for} \quad X > X^*

Where X is perceptual complexity and X^* is the optimal complexity level. This implies:

\boxed{
\text{Boredom} \xrightarrow{\text{increasing complexity}} \text{Wonder} \xrightarrow{\text{continuing increase}} \text{Confusion}
}

This inverted-U relationship can be tested empirically:

\mathcal{W} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X + \beta_2 X^2 + \varepsilon

Where the quadratic term tests the hypothesis that wonder is maximized at intermediate complexity.

