III. The Optimization Problem

The system optimizes its model estimate of wonder, subject to constraints:

\boxed{
\max \mathcal{W}_{\text{model estimate}}
}

Subject to:

\begin{cases}
\text{Risk} \leq R_{\max} & \text{(safety)} \\
C_{\text{model estimate}} \leq C_{\max} & \text{(cognitive load)} \\
\text{Latency} \leq L_{\max} & \text{(system response time)} \\
\text{Energy} \leq E_{\max} & \text{(resource constraints)} \\
\text{AI Autonomy} \leq A_{\max} & \text{(system freedom)} \\
\text{Human Agency} \geq A_{\min} & \text{(agency preservation)}
\end{cases}

The system optimizes its model estimate of wonder, not wonder itself. This distinction is not philosophical; it has practical consequences. The system may be wrong. It may optimize for the wrong things. It may fail.

The goal is not to build a system that always succeeds. The goal is to build a system that can learn from its failures.

---

IV. The Principle of Asymmetric Complexity

\boxed{
\text{Technological Sophistication} \gg \text{Perceived Explanation}
}

The system behind the scenes may be extraordinarily complex, but the experience must remain:

\boxed{
\text{"I don't understand how this is possible."}
}

This asymmetry is the modern equivalent of the magician's oath. It creates a tension: the more the audience understands the system, the less wonder they experience. The less they understand, the more dependent they are on the system's designers for their experience.

This tension is not resolved by the framework. It is a fundamental tension that must be managed—ethically, aesthetically, and scientifically.

But the asymmetry is also empirical. It predicts that the relationship between understanding and wonder is not linear:

\mathcal{W} = f(\text{Understanding})

At zero understanding, wonder may be impossible (the experience is incomprehensible). At complete understanding, wonder may be impossible (the experience is fully explained). Maximum wonder occurs at intermediate understanding:

\boxed{
\text{Wonder is maximized when the audience knows enough to be astonished but not enough to explain.}
}

