VI. The System Dynamics

The performance is a trajectory through a state space defined by the interaction of five agent classes:

\mathcal{S} = \{H, R, AI, E, A\}

Where:

\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{Symbol} & \text{Agent Class} \\
\hline
H & \text{Humans (performers, technicians)} \\
R & \text{Robots (physical agency)} \\
AI & \text{Artificial Intelligence (cognitive agency)} \\
E & \text{Environment (mutable space)} \\
A & \text{Audience (collective)}
\end{array}

The performance is defined by the recursive function:

\mathcal{S}_{t+1} = F(\mathcal{S}_t, A_t, E_t)

Key implications:

1. The show is not written; it is seeded. The creative team designs initial conditions, constraints, and attractors—not a fixed script.
2. The audience is not observing the system; they are inside it. Their state variables are inputs to the system's evolution.
3. The artists are not executing; they are navigating. Their role is co-regulation—guiding the system through desired regions of state space.
4. The system is not autonomous; it is supervised. Human oversight is always present, always able to intervene.

