VII. The Ethical Framework

7.1 The Three Commandments

I. Technology Is Not the Protagonist

\boxed{
\text{Technology} \neq \text{Protagonist}
}

If the audience leaves thinking "that robot was amazing," the project has failed. If they leave thinking "I didn't know that was possible," it has succeeded.

II. Wonder Precedes Explanation

\boxed{
\text{Wonder} > \text{Explanation}
}

The goal is not to educate but to suspend the need for explanation.

III. Human Meaning Transcends Technical Spectacle

\boxed{
\text{Human Meaning} > \text{Technical Spectacle}
}

If a moment of technical brilliance does not serve a deeper human question, it must be cut.

7.2 The Conservation of Human Agency

\boxed{
A_H \geq A_{\min}
}

The system may adapt to the audience, but it may never reduce the audience to an optimization target.

This means:

1. The audience is never deceived about the fact that they are being sensed. They may not know the details, but they must know that their presence affects the performance.
2. The audience always has the capacity to opt out. They can leave. They can disengage. Their participation is voluntary.
3. The system never manipulates the audience toward states they would not choose for themselves. Wonder is not a weapon.
4. The human performers retain ultimate creative authority. The AI may propose, but humans dispose.
5. The system's goals are transparent to the creative team. There are no hidden optimization targets.

7.3 The Deception Protocol

In experimental contexts, deception may be necessary to test specific hypotheses. When deception is used:

1. The deception is minimal—it concerns only the causal mechanism, not the nature of the experience.
2. The deception is disclosed in debriefing.
3. Participants may withdraw their data after learning of the deception.
4. The protocol is reviewed by an independent ethics board.

