VIII. The Experimental Program — AWA-0

8.1 The Research Question

\boxed{
\text{Does adaptive interaction between human performer and machine produce higher subjective wonder than non-adaptive interaction?}
}

This is not a question about co-creation in general. It is a question about a specific, manipulable difference: whether the machine's behavior is contingent on the performer and audience in real-time.

8.2 Experimental Design

Four conditions:

\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{Condition} & \text{Machine Present} & \text{Adaptation Type} \\
\hline
C_1 & \text{No} & \text{None (baseline)} \\
C_2 & \text{Yes} & \text{Scripted} \\
C_3 & \text{Yes} & \text{Adaptive} \\
C_4 & \text{Yes} & \text{Pseudo-Adaptive}
\end{array}

Scripted Machine (C_2): The machine executes a fixed sequence of movements. Its behavior is identical across all sessions. No sensing of performer or audience state.

Adaptive Machine (C_3): The machine's behavior is generated by an algorithm that takes real-time input from performer and audience. The selection function:

\text{Behavior}_t = \arg\max_{b \in \mathcal{B}} \left[ \alpha \cdot \text{Contingency}(b, \text{Input}_t) + (1-\alpha) \cdot \text{Surprise}(b, \text{History}_t) \right]

Where:

\text{Contingency}(b, \text{Input}_t) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(-\beta \cdot d(b, \text{Input}_t))}

\text{Surprise}(b, \text{History}_t) = -\log P(b | \text{History}_t)

Pseudo-Adaptive Machine (C_4): The machine executes a pre-programmed sequence designed to appear adaptive. The sequence has the same statistical properties as C_3 but is fixed across sessions. Audiences are told the machine is adaptive, but this is false.

8.3 Critical Comparisons

\begin{aligned}
C_2 \rightarrow C_3 &: \text{Does adaptation per se increase wonder?} \\
C_3 \rightarrow C_4 &: \text{Does real adaptation differ from apparent adaptation?} \\
C_1 \rightarrow C_2 &: \text{Does machine presence increase wonder?}
\end{aligned}

The C_3 vs C_4 comparison is the methodological innovation. It separates the perception of adaptation from the actuality of adaptation.

If audiences in C_4 report wonder levels similar to C_3, then perceived adaptation is sufficient and actual adaptation is unnecessary. If C_3 exceeds C_4, then something about genuine contingency—the machine's behavior being causally linked to the performer's actions—produces wonder beyond mere appearance.

8.4 Matching Across Conditions

To ensure differences are attributable to adaptation rather than confounds:

· Duration: All conditions are 10 minutes
· Performer: Same performer across all conditions
· Space: Same laboratory space
· Machine: Same robot (in C_2, C_3, C_4)
· Initial movements: Identical for first 2 minutes
· Physical capabilities: Machine's movement envelope identical
· Sound level: Matched
· Visual aesthetics: Matched

The only difference between C_2 and C_3 is whether the machine's behavior is contingent on real-time inputs. The only difference between C_3 and C_4 is whether this contingency is real or simulated.

8.5 Sample Size

For the primary comparison (C_2 vs C_3):

d = 0.5, \quad \alpha = 0.05 \text{ (two-tailed)}, \quad \text{power} = 0.80

n_{\text{per group}} = \frac{2(z_{0.975} + z_{0.80})^2}{0.5^2} = \frac{2(1.96 + 0.84)^2}{0.25} \approx 64

For four conditions:

N_{\text{total}} = 4 \times 64 = 256

8.6 Measurement

Primary Outcome:

\text{SWS} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} \text{item}_j

Secondary Outcomes:

Co-Creation Perception Index (CCPI): Perceived joint production by human and machine agents.

Perceived Impossibility: Degree to which participants felt they could not explain what they were seeing.

Cognitive Load: Self-report plus dual-task performance.

Agency: Whether participants felt respected, not manipulated.

Exploratory Outcomes:

Physiological measures: Heart rate variability, skin conductance, pupil dilation.

Behavioral measures: Gaze direction, posture, vocalization, silence.

Model estimate: The system's real-time estimate of audience wonder.

8.7 Hypotheses

\begin{aligned}
H_1 &: \text{Adaptive interaction produces higher subjective wonder than scripted interaction} \\
H_2 &: \text{Adaptive interaction produces higher perceived co-creation than scripted interaction} \\
H_3 &: \text{The effect of adaptation on wonder is moderated by technology familiarity} \\
H_4 &: \text{The effect of adaptation on wonder is mediated by perceived co-creation} \\
H_5 &: \text{Real adaptation produces higher wonder than pseudo-adaptation}
\end{aligned}

Each hypothesis is independent. The framework is refined by the pattern of successes and failures across the full set.

8.8 Analysis Plan

Primary Analysis:

\text{ANOVA}: \quad \text{SWS} \sim \text{Condition} + \text{Technology Familiarity} + \text{Condition} \times \text{Technology Familiarity}

Planned contrasts:

C_2 \text{ vs } C_3, \quad C_3 \text{ vs } C_4, \quad C_1 \text{ vs } C_2

Mediation Analysis:

\text{Indirect effect} = a \times b

Where a is the effect of adaptation on CCPI and b is the effect of CCPI on SWS controlling for adaptation.

Moderation Analysis:

\text{Simple slopes at } -1\text{SD}, \text{ Mean}, +1\text{SD} \text{ of Technology Familiarity}

Nonlinearity Analysis:

\mathcal{W} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X + \beta_2 X^2 + \varepsilon

8.9 Success Criteria

\begin{cases}
H_1 \text{ supported} & \text{Adaptation increases wonder } (d \geq 0.5) \\
H_2 \text{ supported} & \text{Adaptation increases perceived co-creation} \\
H_5 \text{ supported or rejected informatively} & \text{Real vs. apparent adaptation distinguished} \\
\text{Measurement validated} & \text{Model predicts self-report } (r \geq 0.4) \\
\text{Ethical framework feasible} & \text{Agency preserved under optimization} \\
\text{No safety incidents} & \text{Technical reliability established}
\end{cases}

8.10 Failure Criteria

The framework as a whole would be considered falsified if, across three independent replications:

Criterion 1: No reliable relationship exists between observed response and self-reported wonder (all r < 0.2).

Criterion 2: Adaptation never produces higher wonder than scripted conditions (all d < 0.1).

Criterion 3: Any optimization of wonder inevitably reduces audience agency below acceptable thresholds.

Criterion 4: Cognitive overload always occurs before peak wonder, such that the optimization problem has no feasible solution.

These criteria require repeated failures, not single null results. A single null result may be due to measurement error, inadequate manipulation, or sample characteristics.

