IX. The Adaptation Algorithm — Formal Specification

9.1 The Selection Function

The adaptive machine in C_3 operates according to:

\text{Behavior}_t = \arg\max_{b \in \mathcal{B}} \left[ \alpha \cdot \text{Contingency}(b, \text{Input}_t) + (1-\alpha) \cdot \text{Surprise}(b, \text{History}_t) \right]

Where:

\text{Contingency}(b, \text{Input}_t) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(-\beta \cdot d(b, \text{Input}_t))}

\text{Surprise}(b, \text{History}_t) = -\log P(b | \text{History}_t)

Parameters:

· \alpha = 0.7: Balance between responsiveness and unpredictability
· \beta = 2.0: Sharpness of the contingency function
· |\mathcal{B}| = 12: Number of behaviors in the behavior set

9.2 The Behavior Set

Each behavior is defined by:

· Movement trajectory
· Timing pattern
· Interaction with performer
· Visual effect

The behaviors are designed to be distinguishable by audience members. Each has a clear "signature."

9.3 The Pseudo-Adaptive Sequence

The C_4 sequence is generated by running the adaptation algorithm on a simulated input stream. The simulated input is generated from a model of typical performer and audience behavior.

The resulting sequence has the same statistical properties as C_3 sequences but is fixed across sessions.

9.4 Input Signals

\begin{cases}
\text{Performer Position} & \text{From motion capture (10 Hz)} \\
\text{Performer Velocity} & \text{Derived from position} \\
\text{Audience Silence} & \text{From microphone array (1 Hz)} \\
\text{Audience Movement} & \text{From camera-based tracking (1 Hz)}
\end{cases}

The sensing system is minimal. It tests whether simple adaptation—based on easily measurable signals—can enhance wonder.

