Appendix A — The Wonder Field: Formal Mathematical Treatment

A.1 Axiomatic Foundations

The Wonder Field is defined over a probability space (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, P), where \Omega represents the space of all possible audience states, \mathcal{F} is a \sigma-algebra of measurable events, and P is a probability measure representing the distribution of states across the audience.

Axiom 1 (Latency): The Wonder Field \mathcal{W} is a latent variable. It cannot be directly observed. Only its correlates Y = (Y_1, \ldots, Y_k) are observable.

Axiom 2 (Multidimensionality): The Wonder Field has dimension d \geq 2. It is not a single scalar quantity but a vector:

\mathcal{W} = (\mathcal{W}_1, \mathcal{W}_2, \ldots, \mathcal{W}_d)

Axiom 3 (Temporal Evolution): The Wonder Field evolves over time according to a stochastic process:

\mathcal{W}(t) = \mathcal{W}(0) + \int_0^t \mu(\mathcal{W}(s), U(s)) ds + \int_0^t \sigma(\mathcal{W}(s)) dB(s)

Where \mu is a drift function, U(s) is the control input (system actions), \sigma is a diffusion coefficient, and B(s) is a Brownian motion.

Axiom 4 (Boundedness): The Wonder Field is bounded:

\exists M < \infty : \|\mathcal{W}(t)\| \leq M \quad \forall t

Wonder cannot increase indefinitely. There is a ceiling.

Axiom 5 (Fading): In the absence of input, wonder decays:

\frac{d\mathcal{W}}{dt} = -\gamma \mathcal{W} \quad \text{when } U = 0

Where \gamma > 0 is the decay rate.

A.2 The Component Structure

The Wonder Field decomposes into six components:

\mathcal{W} = (S, P, U, I, E, C)

Each component is a stochastic process:

S(t) = \text{Surprise at time } t

P(t) = \text{Prediction Error at time } t

U(t) = \text{Uncertainty at time } t

I(t) = \text{Emotional Involvement at time } t

E(t) = \text{Emergence at time } t

C(t) = \text{Cognitive Overload at time } t

A.3 The Linear Approximation

For initial experimental work, the Wonder Field is approximated as:

\mathcal{W}(t) = \sum_{i=1}^{5} w_i z_i(t) - \lambda z_6(t)

Where:

z_i(t) = \frac{X_i(t) - \mu_i}{\sigma_i}

are standardized scores with mean 0 and standard deviation 1.

The weights w_i and \lambda are parameters to be estimated from data.

A.4 The Measurement Model

The relationship between the latent Wonder Field and observable indicators:

Y_j = \Lambda_j \mathcal{W} + \varepsilon_j

Where:

· Y_j is the j-th observable indicator (gaze direction, heart rate, self-report)
· \Lambda_j is the loading vector for indicator j
· \varepsilon_j \sim N(0, \sigma_j^2) is measurement error

The self-report measure has a special status:

Y_{\text{self-report}} = \mathcal{W} + \varepsilon_{\text{self-report}}

Where \varepsilon_{\text{self-report}} \sim N(0, \sigma_{\text{self-report}}^2) represents reporting bias, social desirability, and memory distortion.

The model estimate has another status:

\hat{\mathcal{W}} = f(Y_1, \ldots, Y_k; \theta)

Where \theta are model parameters. The estimate is a function of observable indicators, not of the latent field itself.

A.5 The Validation Theorem

Theorem A.1 (Validation Gap): The expected squared error of the model estimate decomposes as:

\mathbb{E}\left[\|\hat{\mathcal{W}} - \mathcal{W}\|^2\right] = \mathbb{E}\left[\|\hat{\mathcal{W}} - \mathbb{E}[\hat{\mathcal{W}}]\|^2\right] + \|\mathbb{E}[\hat{\mathcal{W}}] - \mathcal{W}\|^2

Where the first term is the variance of the estimate and the second is the squared bias.

Proof: Standard bias-variance decomposition. \square

Corollary A.1.1: Even with zero bias, the model estimate cannot perfectly predict the latent field:

\mathbb{E}\left[\|\hat{\mathcal{W}} - \mathcal{W}\|^2\right] \geq \text{Var}(\hat{\mathcal{W}}) > 0

Theorem A.2 (Self-Report Ceiling): The correlation between self-report and the latent field is bounded above:

\rho(Y_{\text{self-report}}, \mathcal{W}) \leq \frac{\text{Var}(\mathcal{W})}{\sqrt{\text{Var}(\mathcal{W}) + \sigma_{\text{self-report}}^2}}

Proof: From the measurement model:

\text{Cov}(Y_{\text{self-report}}, \mathcal{W}) = \text{Cov}(\mathcal{W} + \varepsilon, \mathcal{W}) = \text{Var}(\mathcal{W})

\text{Var}(Y_{\text{self-report}}) = \text{Var}(\mathcal{W}) + \sigma_{\text{self-report}}^2

Therefore:

\rho = \frac{\text{Var}(\mathcal{W})}{\sqrt{\text{Var}(\mathcal{W}) \cdot (\text{Var}(\mathcal{W}) + \sigma_{\text{self-report}}^2)}} = \frac{\text{Var}(\mathcal{W})}{\sqrt{\text{Var}(\mathcal{W}) + \sigma_{\text{self-report}}^2}}

Since \sigma_{\text{self-report}}^2 > 0, the correlation is strictly less than 1. \square

Implication: Even with perfect self-report (zero bias), the correlation between self-report and the latent field is less than 1 because of measurement error. The validation threshold of r \geq 0.4 accounts for this ceiling.

A.6 The Nonlinearity Theorem

Theorem A.3 (Inverted-U): Under the model:

\mathcal{W} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X + \beta_2 X^2 + \varepsilon

with \beta_2 < 0, the Wonder Field is maximized at:

X^* = -\frac{\beta_1}{2\beta_2}

Proof: First-order condition:

\frac{d\mathcal{W}}{dX} = \beta_1 + 2\beta_2 X = 0

X^* = -\frac{\beta_1}{2\beta_2}

Second-order condition:

\frac{d^2\mathcal{W}}{dX^2} = 2\beta_2 < 0

Therefore X^* is a maximum. \square

Corollary A.3.1: If \beta_2 < 0, there exists an optimal complexity level X^* > 0 such that:

\mathcal{W}(X) < \mathcal{W}(X^*) \quad \forall X \neq X^*

