Appendix B — The Adaptation Algorithm: Convergence and Optimality

B.1 The Selection Function

The adaptive machine selects behaviors according to:

\text{Behavior}_t = \arg\max_{b \in \mathcal{B}} \left[ \alpha \cdot C(b, \text{Input}_t) + (1-\alpha) \cdot S(b, \text{History}_t) \right]

Where:

C(b, \text{Input}_t) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(-\beta \cdot d(b, \text{Input}_t))}

S(b, \text{History}_t) = -\log P(b | \text{History}_t)

B.2 Properties

Theorem B.1 (Bounded Selection): The selection function always selects a behavior from the finite set \mathcal{B}:

\forall t: \text{Behavior}_t \in \mathcal{B}

Proof: The argmax is over the finite set \mathcal{B}. Therefore the selected behavior is an element of \mathcal{B}. \square

Theorem B.2 (Contingency Monotonicity): The contingency function C(b, \text{Input}_t) is monotonically decreasing in the distance d(b, \text{Input}_t):

d(b_1, \text{Input}) < d(b_2, \text{Input}) \implies C(b_1, \text{Input}) > C(b_2, \text{Input})

Proof: The logistic function is monotonically increasing in its argument. The argument is -\beta \cdot d(b, \text{Input}), which is monotonically decreasing in d. The composition of a decreasing function with an increasing function is decreasing. \square

Theorem B.3 (Surprise Boundedness): The surprise function is bounded below:

S(b, \text{History}_t) \geq 0

Proof: Since P(b | \text{History}_t) \in (0, 1], we have -\log P(b | \text{History}_t) \geq 0. \square

Theorem B.4 (Exploration-Exploitation Tradeoff): The parameter \alpha controls the tradeoff between contingency (exploitation of current input) and surprise (exploration of novel behaviors):

\alpha = 1 \implies \text{pure contingency (always respond to input)}

\alpha = 0 \implies \text{pure surprise (always seek novelty)}

Proof: Direct substitution into the selection function. \square

B.3 Convergence Properties

Theorem B.5 (Finite State Space): The behavior sequence is a Markov chain on the finite state space \mathcal{B}:

P(\text{Behavior}_{t+1} = b' | \text{Behavior}_t = b, \text{History}_{t-1}) = P(\text{Behavior}_{t+1} = b' | \text{History}_t)

Proof: The selection at time t+1 depends only on the current input and the history of behaviors, not on the path by which the history was reached. \square

Theorem B.6 (Ergodicity): If \alpha < 1 and the surprise function is strictly positive for all behaviors not recently selected, the behavior sequence is ergodic:

\lim_{t \to \infty} P(\text{Behavior}_t = b) = \pi(b) > 0 \quad \forall b \in \mathcal{B}

Proof sketch: The surprise term ensures that no behavior has zero probability of selection indefinitely. The finite state space and positive transition probabilities imply irreducibility and aperiodicity, which are sufficient for ergodicity. \square

Theorem B.7 (Convergence Rate): The convergence rate to the stationary distribution is geometric:

\|P^t - \pi\|_{\text{TV}} \leq C \cdot \rho^t

Where C is a constant and \rho < 1 is the spectral gap of the transition matrix.

Proof: Standard result for finite Markov chains. \square

B.4 The Pseudo-Adaptive Sequence

Theorem B.8 (Statistical Indistinguishability): The pseudo-adaptive sequence (C_4) is statistically indistinguishable from the adaptive sequence (C_3) to an observer who cannot access the input stream.

Proof: Both sequences are generated by the same algorithm. The only difference is whether the input is real or simulated. To an observer without access to the input, the sequences have the same distribution. \square

Corollary B.8.1: Any difference in audience response between C_3 and C_4 cannot be attributed to observable differences in machine behavior. It must be attributed to the causal relationship between the machine's behavior and the performer's actions.

