Appendix D — Measurement Instrument Validation

D.1 The Subjective Wonder Score (SWS)

Item Pool:

The SWS is adapted from the Awe Experience Scale (AWE-S). Items are selected to assess:

1. Felt wonder ("I felt a sense of wonder")
2. Perceived vastness ("I felt in the presence of something greater than myself")
3. Need for accommodation ("I had difficulty comprehending the experience")
4. Transcendence ("I felt transported beyond my everyday concerns")

Item Format:

Each item is rated on a 7-point Likert scale:

1 = \text{"Strongly disagree"} \quad \text{to} \quad 7 = \text{"Strongly agree"}

Proposed Items:

1. "I felt a sense of wonder during the performance."
2. "The performance made me question what is possible."
3. "I could not explain what I was seeing."
4. "I felt that new things were emerging from the interaction."
5. "I felt in the presence of something greater than myself."
6. "The experience challenged my understanding of the world."
7. "I felt transported beyond my everyday concerns."
8. "The performance made me see the world differently."

Scoring:

\text{SWS} = \frac{1}{8} \sum_{j=1}^{8} \text{item}_j

D.2 The Co-Creation Perception Index (CCPI)

Construct Definition:

Perceived co-creation is the degree to which an observer perceives that a performance emerged from the genuine joint production of human and machine agents, rather than from the execution of a pre-determined plan by either agent alone.

Proposed Items:

1. "The machine and the performer were creating something together."
2. "The machine was responding to the performer in real-time."
3. "The performance emerged from the interaction between human and machine."
4. "The machine was an active participant, not just a tool."
5. "The performer and the machine were in dialogue."
6. "The outcome could not have been predicted from the individual contributions."

Scoring:

\text{CCPI} = \frac{1}{6} \sum_{j=1}^{6} \text{item}_j

D.3 Perceived Impossibility Scale

Construct Definition:

Perceived impossibility is the degree to which an observer feels unable to explain how an observed phenomenon could occur.

Proposed Items:

1. "I could not explain what I was seeing."
2. "The performance violated my expectations about what is possible."
3. "I was unable to figure out how the effects were produced."
4. "The performance exceeded my understanding of the technology involved."

D.4 Cognitive Load Measure

Self-Report Items:

1. "I felt mentally overwhelmed during the performance."
2. "The performance required too much attention to follow."
3. "I had difficulty keeping up with what was happening."

Dual-Task Performance:

During the performance, participants are asked to press a button when they hear a tone. Reaction time and accuracy are measured. Increased cognitive load is associated with slower reaction times and lower accuracy.

D.5 Agency Measure

Proposed Items:

1. "I felt respected during the performance."
2. "I felt that I could leave if I wanted to."
3. "I did not feel manipulated."
4. "I felt that my presence mattered to the performance."

D.6 Validation Procedures

Reliability:

Internal consistency (Cronbach's \alpha):

\alpha = \frac{k}{k-1} \left(1 - \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k} \sigma_i^2}{\sigma_{\text{total}}^2}\right)

Target: \alpha \geq 0.80 for all scales.

Construct Validity:

Convergent validity: Correlation with established measures (AWE-S, awe subscale of DPES).

Discriminant validity: Low correlation with unrelated constructs (e.g., boredom, anxiety).

Factor Structure:

Confirmatory factor analysis (CFA) to verify that items load on their intended factors:

\chi^2 / df < 3, \quad \text{CFI} > 0.90, \quad \text{RMSEA} < 0.08

