Appendix F — Safety Protocols: Complete Specifications

F.1 Technical Safety

Emergency Stop:

The robot is equipped with an emergency stop button accessible to:

· The performer
· The research staff
· A designated safety officer

The emergency stop immediately halts all robot movement.

Redundant Power:

Critical systems (safety monitoring, emergency stop) have redundant power supplies.

Monitoring:

Research staff monitor the performance in real-time. Any anomaly triggers a review and potential intervention.

Graceful Degradation:

If any system fails, the performance degrades gracefully:

· Projection failure: Physical performance continues
· Audio failure: Visual performance continues
· Sensing failure: System falls back to scripted behavior
· Robot failure: Performer continues solo

F.2 Cognitive Safety

Overload Indicators:

Real-time monitoring of:

· Self-reported cognitive load (post-session)
· Behavioral indicators (confusion, disengagement)
· Physiological indicators (if available)

Overload Thresholds:

C_{\text{model estimate}} \leq C_{\max} = 0.75 \text{ (standardized)}

If the threshold is exceeded, the system automatically reduces complexity.

Human Override:

The performer can simplify the experience at any time:

· Slow the pace
· Repeat movements
· Reduce complexity
· End the performance early

F.3 Ethical Safety

Consent Protocol:

Participants receive:

1. Information about the study purpose
2. Description of the performance
3. Disclosure of sensing practices
4. Description of data collection
5. Explanation of withdrawal rights
6. Contact information for questions

Consent Levels:

\begin{cases}
\text{Level 1} & \text{Presence detection (is someone there?)} \\
\text{Level 2} & \text{Behavioral sensing (gaze, movement, vocalization)} \\
\text{Level 3} & \text{Physiological sensing (heart rate, skin conductance)}
\end{cases}

Participants may decline Level 3 without being excluded. They may withdraw at any time.

Debriefing Protocol:

After the session:

1. Full disclosure of condition assignment
2. Explanation of the deception (if applicable)
3. Opportunity to ask questions
4. Opportunity to withdraw data
5. Contact information for follow-up

Data Protection:

All data are anonymized. Identifiable information is stored separately and destroyed after the study.

Ethics Review:

The protocol is reviewed by an independent ethics board before data collection begins. The board has authority to:

· Approve the protocol
· Require modifications
· Reject the protocol
· Monitor the study
· Suspend the study if concerns arise

